  "catalog": "This course explores the primary approaches for developing computer programs that display characteristics we would think of as being intelligent. Students will analyze how intelligent systems are developed and implemented with a focus on exploring how human behavior on cognitive tasks can be used to inform the development of these artificial systems, as well as how the performance and behavior of these artificial systems can inform our understanding of human cognition.",
  "goals": [
    {
      "num": 1,
      "action": "Represent",
      "detail": "knowledge and search spaces using symbolic AI structures and problem-solving algorithms.",
      "blooms": 3
    },
    {
      "num": 2,
      "action": "Analyze",
      "detail": "human cognitive tasks, memory models, and learning mechanisms to inform artificial agent design.",
      "blooms": 4
    },
    {
      "num": 3,
      "action": "Evaluate",
      "detail": "cognitive agent performance, case-based reasoning, and metacognitive error-correction strategies.",
      "blooms": 5
    },
    {
      "num": 4,
      "action": "Design",
      "detail": "an integrated cognitive agent combining search, memory, and reasoning to solve complex tasks.",
      "blooms": 6
    }
